A||||7||jhogans||Interesting Non-Fiero Kits!!!!||||14||
Z||000000||jhogans||04-09-2001||05:23 PM||||I picked up the latest copy of "Kit Car - The Car Builder's Authority" magazine and I saw 2 cool looking kits.  One kit turns a 1988-1991 Honda Civic hatchback into a rebodied convertable.  The other kits turns an early 80's to 1900 full-size GM four-door car (Caprice, Bonneville, Olds 98) into somewhat modern El Camino.  You got to see the pictures in the magazine to appreciate the style. <P>Here are the websites: <A HREF="http://www.superstepside.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.superstepside.com</A>  - these pictures are terrible quality. <A HREF="http://www.vitessesportscar.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.vitessesportscar.com</A> <P>Anybody know of any other kits that are not Fiero and VW based, or Cobras? <P>||||reg||14
Z||000001||FieroLisa||04-09-2001||05:49 PM||||I think there were 2 of those el camino things at the Daytona show...||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhogans||04-09-2001||06:02 PM||||FieroLisa,<P>How did they look in person.  They look awesome in the magazine.  The website didn't do it justice at all.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||04-09-2001||06:20 PM||||Somebody actually <I>wants</I> to convert their cars into El Camino's?  Atleast the Fiero community goes after Ferrari's and Lambo's! hehe||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-09-2001||07:45 PM||||Lisa is right, there were two at Daytona and they did look sharp.  You gotta like the Elcamino look.||||reg||1
